  body: "# Primary evidence of age, marriage or death\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe source identifies certified
    statutory-register entries as a primary evidential route, while allowing discrepancies and strong contrary evidence
    to be considered.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nA certificate is not infallible and a photocopy is not automatically
    equivalent. Foreign certificates and identity discrepancies have specific qualifications. The full territorial
    and historical marriage provisions are outside this batch.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 10030,
    PDF page 5](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=5) — DMG
    Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10032, PDF page 6](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=6)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10033, PDF page 6](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=6)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n- [DMG 10034, PDF page 6](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/681c8caa9ef97b58cce3e5a4/dmgch10.pdf#page=6)
    — DMG Vol 3 Ch 10: Evidence of age, marriage and death .\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted
    interpretation of the 15 September 2026 source snapshot; unreviewed and requiring specialist review. The source
    is DWP guidance, not a consolidated statement of legislation. This is an independent experimental publication,
    not an official DWP document, an entitlement decision or an executable benefits rule.\n\nPDF page numbers are
    one-based file positions. Machine extraction can alter reading order, footnotes and characters. Quoted relationships
    preserve extracted text; no visual or specialist acceptance is claimed. Source dates, legal effects and current
    applicability remain separate review tasks.\n\n- `dmg-vol3-ch10` PDF SHA-256: `e87cd0079de36002a9ae5d065256197eccce3b9257537a29da5bd3220145021a`;
    extracted pages SHA-256: `0fdc816b2a57b058f8826c36ac756cd414da56c94f580a9b5b32e0ff874a68b8`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-civil-status-secondary-evidence
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The passage compares the evidential weight and handling of secondary material when primary
      evidence is unavailable; the two types are not interchangeable.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol3-ch10/0006
      quote: "10036 If primary evidence is not available, the DM should ask for the claimant’s observations before\n\
        making a decision. Unsupported secondary evidence carries less weight than primary evidence. The DM\nshould
        ensure that as much information as possible has been obtained, before considering the relative\nvalue of
        each piece of evidence."
      locator: DMG 10036
